  "prs": [
    {
      "number": 14516,
      "title": "[css-overflow-4] Clarify the interaction between `block-ellipsis` and `text-overflow`",
      "url": "https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/pull/14516",
      "author": "andreubotella",
      "mergedAt": "2026-10-07T13:35:52Z",
      "labels": [
        "css-overflow-4",
        "Needs Testcase (WPT)",
        "Closed Accepted by Editor Discretion",
        "topic: line-clamp"
      ],
      "hint": "unknown",
      "release": "⏳ 未リリース",
      "body": "In #10823, we resolved that `line-clamp` does not block the effects of `text-overflow`. In the discussion, it was mentioned that if both the line-clamp ellipsis (as set by the `block-ellipsis` longhand) and the `text-overflow` ellipsis would apply to the same line, the line-clamp ellipsis would apply first; so no spec change was needed.\n\nHowever, the spec is not necessarily clear about that behavior. It does say that `text-overflow` must not affect layout, but that's not the same as saying that it comes *after* `block-ellipsis`. This is especially true because in some implementations, the old behavior of the `-webkit-line-clamp` ellipsis happened after layout; and in other implementations, the `text-overflow` ellipsis itself was placed as part of layout.\n\nGiven this, it made sense to explicitly specify that `block-ellipsis` applies first; and `text-overflow` only applies afterwards, if the line still overflows.",
      "files": [
        "css-overflow-4/Overview.bs (+8 -0)"
      ],
      "filesTotal": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 14556,
      "title": "[cssom-view-1] Resolve scroll promises with ScrollResult dictionary (#12495)",
      "url": "https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/pull/14556",
      "author": "canalun",
      "mergedAt": "2026-10-07T23:04:36Z",
      "hint": "unknown",
      "release": "⏳ 未リリース",
      "body": "Resolve scroll promises with a `ScrollResult` dictionary indicating whether the scroll was interrupted.\n\nWhile #12495 raised up the three points, this PR only addresses the first point (i.e., what the promises are resolved with), because the second and third points of #12495 are already reflected in the spec.\n\nThis PR specifies that...\n1. if a smooth scroll is aborted by an algorithm, its promise is resolved with `interrupted: true`.\n2. if a smooth scroll is aborted by a user interaction, its promise is resolved with `interrupted: true`.\n3. in any other cases such as when the element has no associated scrolling box, its promise is resolved with `interrupted: false`.\n\nFixes #12495",
      "files": [
        "cssom-view-1/Overview.bs (+39 -29)"
      ],
      "filesTotal": 1
    },
